From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Jun 94 01:42:36 -0700
Subject: [#94-6-115] digital cash and free banking 

Dave,

All your arguments in favor of an arbitrary currency standard with a
"frozen monetary base" and no central-bank manipulation are cogent,
and I certainly don't intend to argue with them. I just have one
questions: once frozen, how does one *keep* the monetary base frozen?
There are monitoring issues here that I'm not sure the market can
handle. Of course, random fluctuations and market-responsive
fluctuations can be redressed by market-responsive changes in the
amount of credit issued by banks, but without the physical commodity
link, what's to prevent manipulation of the monetary base by the
biggest banks?  As you correctly point out, none of this is relevant
to the issue of digital cash, which is anonymity.

ewf> in all U. S. jurisdictions, you must get official permission from
ewf> the state to open a new bank. Such permission is very difficult
ewf> to get in most states. No such permission is required (in most
ewf> states) to open a new barter exchange or commodity depository.
ewf> So backing digital cash with anything of value other than fiat
ewf> currency would expose one to far less bureaucratic oversight.

TDB> I find this reasoning much more persuasive. This even seems like
TDB> a possible path to separating the unit of account from the medium
TDB> of exchange. But doesn't the IRS keep a pretty enthusiastic eye
TDB> on barter exchanges, and aren't there regs on precious metals
TDB> dealers, etc? What unit do barter exchanges use for accounting?

The IRS requires barter exchanges to report all exchanges above a
certain dollar value, which I think is in the high hundreds or low
thousands. Other than this reporting requirement (which kills
anonymity unless the barter exchange requires all transactions to
be "fine grained", which the IRS could in turn interpret as illegal
"structuring" of payments, which I don't even want to get into), the
regs on barter exchanges are very light. And regular banks certainly
have much stricter reporting requirements than barter exchanges do.

The regs on commodity depositories (to be carefully distinguished from
commodities *traders* and *brokers*) are much lighter... nearly 
nonexistent at the federal level. There are no reporting requirements
on commodities changing hands at them (as long as they are not
functioning as barter exchanges) that I know of... meaning that the
burden of reporting rests solely on the taxpayer in question, not
on the depository.

The downside with commodities depositories is that the industry as 
a whole has a very poor reputation, and reputation is essential for
a digicash-backing repository to be commercially successful. Financial
advisors are always quick to relate stories of gold depositories that
disappeared in the night with their customers valuta. While this is
a burden on our hypothetical digicash-issuing business, I think it is
a lesser burden than the regulations on banks and barter exchanges.

A digicash-issuer that backs its notes with deposited commodities will
have to work hard to establish a sterling (excuse the pun) reputation
among its customers and potential customers, but really, we knew that
already, didn't we?

Finally: I'm sure that regs will be issued to cover these "loopholes"
once the hypothesized new digicash industry has been established, but
*establishing* it is the hard part... once the "loopholes" are closed,
the industry can move offshore, its customer base (relatively) intact.

